      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Lung disease associated with marked peripheral blood eosinophilia is unusual and nearly always clinically significant. Once recognized, it is generally easy to manage, albeit with long-term systemic corticosteroids. A failure to respond to oral steroids in the context of good compliance suggests a malignant cause for the eosinophilia. An important development is the introduction of antieosinophil therapies, particularly those directed against the interleukin 5 pathway, which is hoped to provide benefit in the full spectrum of eosinophilic lung disease as well as asthma, reducing the burden of side effects and resultant comorbidities."
      },
